{
  "assessment": "The source packet provides a single coherent quantitative cluster: the unitary system-bath entanglement transition in a 2D free-fermion lattice, with the connected correlation weight C(gamma, L) fitted as c(gamma,L) x ln(x) + b(gamma,L) x over x in [8, L], and finite-size scaling yielding critical exponents and gamma_c. The selected controls are the system-bath coupling rate gamma and the linear system size L; the readout is the connected correlation weight C. The source reports two regimes (logarithmic-law at gamma=0.1J and area-law at gamma>=0.4J) and a critical point gamma_c=(0.13±0.01)J. The extraction includes the fitted coefficients c and b as parameters, the FSS exponents, and the fixed conditions (omega_max=10J, h_s=5J, M=100, t_s=50/J). The case is authoring-ready because it supports a numeric control (gamma), a numeric readout (C), a quantitative mechanism (the fit equation), and an objective source-grounded response within an explicit domain.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Bo Xing, Entanglement Transition in Unitary System-Bath Dynamics, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/7g5s-hsd6",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/7g5s-hsd6",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2512.06081v3"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper studies entanglement transitions in a 2D lattice of free fermions coupled to fermionic baths, comparing quantum-trajectory unraveling of a GKSL master equation with a fully unitary system-bath evolution. The selected cluster focuses on the unitary evolution, where the steady-state bipartite logarithmic fermionic negativity, mutual information, and connected correlation weight exhibit a transition from logarithmic-law to area-law scaling as the system-bath coupling rate gamma increases. The transition is characterized by finite-size scaling of the connected correlation weight C(gamma, L).",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "unitary_system_bath_evolution",
      "statement": "The total system-bath Hamiltonian H_tot = H_S + H_B + H_SB is quadratic in fermionic operators, so the unitary evolution preserves Gaussianity and the total correlation matrix C_0 evolves unitarily in time. The system-bath coupling is V(omega_m) = sqrt(gamma * omega_m / M * omega_max / (pi * h_s)).",
      "equations": [
        "H_tot = H_S + H_B + H_SB",
        "H_B = sum_n sum_m omega_m b^dagger_{n,m} b_{n,m}",
        "H_SB = - sum_n sum_m V(omega_m) (a^dagger_n b_{n,m} + H.c.)",
        "V(omega_m) = sqrt(gamma * omega_m / M * omega_max / (pi * h_s))"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "H_tot",
        "H_S",
        "H_B",
        "H_SB",
        "omega_m",
        "V(omega_m)",
        "gamma",
        "M",
        "omega_max",
        "h_s"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "V_coupling_prefactor",
          "value": 1.0,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Proportionality constant in the system-bath coupling V(omega_m) = sqrt(gamma * omega_m / M * omega_max / (pi * h_s))",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 177,
              "line_end": 177,
              "support": "The system-bath coupling has to be V(omega_m) = sqrt(gamma * omega_m / M * omega_max / (pi * h_s))."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 164,
          "line_end": 173,
          "support": "Since H_tot is quadratic in the fermionic operators, the unitary evolution preserves Gaussianity. The total correlation matrix C_0 evolves unitarily in time t under U(t) = e^{-i H_tot t}."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 177,
          "line_end": 177,
          "support": "To recover C^SS = C^ME, M and omega_max must be sufficiently large, and the system-bath coupling has to be V(omega_m) = sqrt(gamma * omega_m / M * omega_max / (pi * h_s))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "connected_correlation_weight_fit",
      "statement": "The connected correlation weight C(gamma, x) is fitted as c(gamma, L) x ln(x) + b(gamma, L) x within the range x in [8, L], where c and b are the logarithmic correction and area-law coefficients, respectively. Finite-size scaling of c(gamma, L) uses the form c(gamma, L) = L^{zeta/nu} f(L^{1/nu} (gamma - gamma_c)).",
      "equations": [
        "C(gamma, x) = c(gamma, L) x ln(x) + b(gamma, L) x",
        "c(gamma, L) = L^{zeta/nu} f(L^{1/nu} (gamma - gamma_c))"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "C",
        "c",
        "b",
        "x",
        "L",
        "gamma",
        "zeta",
        "nu",
        "gamma_c"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "c_log_coefficient",
          "value": 1.0,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Coefficient of x ln(x) in the fit of C(gamma, x); its value is obtained by fitting and is not a fixed constant.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 241,
              "line_end": 241,
              "support": "For each gamma, we obtain b(gamma, L) and c(gamma, L) by fitting the connected correlation weight C(gamma, x) = c(gamma, L) x ln(x) + b(gamma, L) x within different ranges of linear system sizes, x in [8, L]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "b_area_coefficient",
          "value": 1.0,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Coefficient of x in the fit of C(gamma, x); its value is obtained by fitting and is not a fixed constant.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 241,
              "line_end": 241,
              "support": "For each gamma, we obtain b(gamma, L) and c(gamma, L) by fitting the connected correlation weight C(gamma, x) = c(gamma, L) x ln(x) + b(gamma, L) x within different ranges of linear system sizes, x in [8, L]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "zeta_critical_exponent",
          "value": 0.0,
          "uncertainty": "0.01",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Critical exponent zeta in the finite-size scaling form c(gamma, L) = L^{zeta/nu} f(L^{1/nu} (gamma - gamma_c)).",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 250,
              "line_end": 250,
              "support": "yielding critical exponents zeta = 0.00 ± 0.01, nu = 1.26 ± 0.48, and gamma_c = (0.13 ± 0.01)J."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "nu_critical_exponent",
          "value": 1.26,
          "uncertainty": "0.48",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Critical exponent nu in the finite-size scaling form c(gamma, L) = L^{zeta/nu} f(L^{1/nu} (gamma - gamma_c)).",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 250,
              "line_end": 250,
              "support": "yielding critical exponents zeta = 0.00 ± 0.01, nu = 1.26 ± 0.48, and gamma_c = (0.13 ± 0.01)J."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "gamma_c_critical_point",
          "value": 0.13,
          "uncertainty": "0.01",
          "unit": "J",
          "role": "Critical point in the thermodynamic limit for the entanglement transition, in units of J.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 250,
              "line_end": 250,
              "support": "yielding critical exponents zeta = 0.00 ± 0.01, nu = 1.26 ± 0.48, and gamma_c = (0.13 ± 0.01)J."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 241,
          "line_end": 241,
          "support": "For each gamma, we obtain b(gamma, L) and c(gamma, L) by fitting the connected correlation weight C(gamma, x) = c(gamma, L) x ln(x) + b(gamma, L) x within different ranges of linear system sizes, x in [8, L]."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 249,
          "line_end": 250,
          "support": "We perform a finite size scaling (FSS) analysis with the pyfssa package and fit the behavior of c(gamma, L) with the function c(gamma, L) = L^{zeta/nu} f(L^{1/nu} (gamma - gamma_c)), where zeta, nu are the critical exponents, and gamma_c is the critical point in the thermodynamic limit. In Fig. M_Corr(c), we observe that the curves for different system sizes collapse nicely onto each other, yielding critical exponents zeta = 0.00 ± 0.01, nu = 1.26 ± 0.48, and gamma_c = (0.13 ± 0.01)J."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "fixed_conditions": [
    {
      "condition_id": "unitary_setup_parameters",
      "statement": "For all unitary system-bath evolutions, omega_max = 10J, h_s = 5J, and M = 100.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 178,
          "line_end": 178,
          "support": "For all unitary system-bath evolutions, we use omega_max=10J, h_s=5J, and M=100."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "steady_state_time",
      "statement": "The steady-state time is t_s = 50/J.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 182,
          "line_end": 182,
          "support": "At t ≳ t_s, C^SS converges to the same featureless infinite-temperature state as C^ME, but the bath contains non-trivial correlations that are built up through the interaction with the system."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 398,
          "line_end": 398,
          "support": "At large time t_s = 50/J, both C^SS and C^ME converge to the infinite-temperature state, i.e., C^SS(t_s) = C^ME(t_s) = 1/2."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "initial_conditions",
      "statement": "At t=0, C^SS is in a checkerboard configuration and C^BB is the infinite-temperature mixed state.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 180,
          "line_end": 180,
          "support": "At t = 0, C^SS is in a checkerboard configuration and C^BB is the infinite-temperature mixed state."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "fit_range",
      "statement": "The connected correlation weight C(gamma, x) is fitted within the range x in [8, L].",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 241,
          "line_end": 241,
          "support": "For each gamma, we obtain b(gamma, L) and c(gamma, L) by fitting the connected correlation weight C(gamma, x) = c(gamma, L) x ln(x) + b(gamma, L) x within different ranges of linear system sizes, x in [8, L]."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "controls": [
    {
      "name": "gamma",
      "definition": "System-bath coupling rate, appearing in the system-bath coupling V(omega_m) = sqrt(gamma * omega_m / M * omega_max / (pi * h_s)).",
      "unit": "J",
      "domain_kind": "continuous_interval",
      "minimum": 0.1,
      "maximum": 0.4,
      "allowed_values": [],
      "domain_statement": "The paper reports results for gamma = 0.1J (logarithmic-law) and gamma >= 0.4J (area-law), and the critical point is gamma_c = (0.13 ± 0.01)J. The transition occurs as gamma increases, so the relevant interval is [0.1J, 0.4J].",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 207,
          "line_end": 209,
          "support": "In the main panels, the results for gamma >= 0.4J exhibit a clear area-law scaling, where all three quantities scale linearly with L. At gamma = 0.1J, the scaling is distinctly superlinear with L. In the insets, we investigate the scaling behavior at gamma = 0.1J and find that it follows a logarithmic-law scaling, increasing linearly with L log(L)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 250,
          "line_end": 250,
